 mystique to it. He began by interviewing the suspect, Aubrey.\n\nWalking into the office of Aubrey was like walking into a different time period. The room was grand and ornate, filled with all types of frames and decor, managed by none other than Aubrey himself, the boss of the esteemed business.\n\nWinston started the conversation careful not to rush. \"You designed the labyrinth?\" he asked, nodding.\n\nA look of pride crossed Aubrey's face, \"Yes, indeed. I even supervised its construction.\"\n\nWinston casually inquired, \"And the labyrinth is part of the business property, I presume?\"\n\n\"Most definitely. It's a showcase, so to speak,\" Aubrey said.\n\nWinston continued the conversation but his attention was drawn by a box in a display case on Aubrey's desk. The box was labeled \"Keepsake\". \"What's in the box?\" he asked.\n\nAubrey looked slightly taken aback before he smiled, \"Oh, that. It's a lock of hair from someone who owed me. This person owed me and this is my keepsake,\" he admitted with an air of nonchalance.\n\nA brief silence followed. Breaking it, Winston continued, \"The labyrinth. Pretty difficult to navigate, I assume. Any blueprints or maps for it?\"\n\nAubrey half shook his head, \"The labyrinth is an enigma, detective, designed to challenge and confound. There are no public maps or blueprints for it.\"\n\nWinston couldn't help but notice a defaced picture in Aubrey's collection. It was a photo of a once close friend, the eyes had been scratched out thoroughly. \"What happened to the picture?\" Winston asked. \n\nAubrey didn't take a second to respond, \"A moment of rage. I was angry at betrayal by an old friend. I scratched the eyes out and after the argument, I put it back in there.\" \n\nLater on, Winston found out about a whispered overheard conversation Aubrey had about Lauren's betrayal as Aubrey was outside Lauren's house. Apparently, Aubrey had overheard a conversation between Lauren and a competitor. This lead to the argument and